\phi_{\text{outside}}=\frac{\rho a^3}{3\epsilon_0 r}.

\end{equation}

For points inside, the field is due only to the charge $Q(r)$ inside

the sphere of radius $r$, $Q(r)=4\pi r^3\rho/3$, so

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:12:24}

E=\frac{\rho r}{3\epsilon_0}.

\end{equation}

The field increases linearly with $r$. Integrating $E$ to get $\phi$,

we have

\begin{equation*}

\phi_{\text{inside}}=-\frac{\rho r^2}{6\epsilon_0}+\text{a constant}.

\end{equation*}

At the radius $a$, $\phi_{\text{inside}}$ must be the same

