fact, $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}'=\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}+\nabla^2\psi$, and by a suitable

choice of $\psi$ we can make $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}'$ anything we wish.

What should we choose for $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}$? The choice should be made

to get the greatest mathematical convenience and will depend on the

problem we are doing. For magnetostatics , we will make the

simple choice

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:6}

\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}=0.

\end{equation}

(Later, when we take up electrodynamics, we will change our choice.)

Our complete definition 1

of $\mathbf{A}$ is then, for the moment, $\nabla \times \mathbf{A}=\mathbf{B}$

and $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}=0$.

To get some experience with the vector potential, let’s look first at

what it is for a uniform magnetic field $\mathbf{B}_0$. Taking our $z$-axis

in the direction of $\mathbf{B}_0$, we must have

\begin{equation}

\begin{alignedat}{4}

&B_x&&=\frac{\partial A_z}{\partial y}&&-\frac{\partial A_y}{\partial z}&&=0,\\[1ex]

&B_y&&=\frac{\partial A_x}{\partial z}&&-\frac{\partial A_z}{\partial x}&&=0,\\[1ex]

&B_z&&=\frac{\partial A_y}{\partial x}&&-\frac{\partial A_x}{\partial y}&&=B_0.\\

\end{alignedat}

\label{Eq:II:14:7}

\end{equation}

By inspection, we see that one possible solution of these

equations is

\begin{equation*}

A_y=xB_0,\quad A_x=0,\quad A_z=0.

\end{equation*}

Or we could equally well take

\begin{equation*}

A_x=-yB_0,\quad A_y=0,\quad A_z=0.

\end{equation*}

Still another solution is a linear combination of the two:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:8}

A_x=-\frac{1}{2}yB_0,\quad A_y=\frac{1}{2}xB_0,\quad A_z=0.

\end{equation}

It is clear that for any particular field $\mathbf{B}$, the vector

potential $\mathbf{A}$ is not unique; there are many possibilities.

The third solution, Eq. ( 14.8 ), has some interesting

properties. Since the $x$-component is proportional to $-y$ and the

$y$-component is proportional to $+x$, $\mathbf{A}$ must be at right angles

to the vector from the $z$-axis, which we will call $\mathbf{r}'$ (the

“prime” is to remind us that it is not the vector

displacement from the origin). Also, the magnitude of $\mathbf{A}$ is

proportional to $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and, hence, to $r'$. So $\mathbf{A}$ can

be simply written (for our uniform field) as

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:9}

\mathbf{A}=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{B}_0\times\mathbf{r}'.

\end{equation}

The vector potential $\mathbf{A}$ has the magnitude $B_0r'/2$ and rotates

about the $z$-axis as shown in Fig.  14-1 . If, for

example, the $\mathbf{B}$ field is the axial field inside a solenoid, then

the vector potential circulates in the same sense as do the currents

of the solenoid.

[Chapter 14. The Magnetic Field in Various Situations > 14-1 The vector potential]

of the solenoid.

Fig. 14-1. A uniform magnetic field $\mathbf{B}$ in the $z$-direction

corresponds to a vector potential $\mathbf{A}$ that rotates about the

$z$-axis, with the magnitude $A=Br'/2$ ($r'$ is the displacement

from the $z$-axis).

The vector potential for a uniform field can be obtained in another

way. The circulation of $\mathbf{A}$ on any closed loop $\Gamma$ can be

related to the surface integral of $\nabla \times \mathbf{A}$ by Stokes’

theorem, Eq. ( 3.38 ):

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:10}

\oint_\Gamma\mathbf{A}\cdot d\mathbf{s}=

\underset{\text{inside $\Gamma$}}{\int}

(\nabla \times \mathbf{A})\cdot\mathbf{n}\,da.

\end{equation}

But the integral on the right is equal to the flux of $\mathbf{B}$ through

the loop, so

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:11}

\oint_\Gamma\mathbf{A}\cdot d\mathbf{s}=

\underset{\text{inside $\Gamma$}}{\int}

\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{n}\,da.

\end{equation}

So the circulation of $\mathbf{A}$ around any loop is equal to the

flux of $\mathbf{B}$ through the loop. If we take a circular loop, of

radius $r'$ in a plane perpendicular to a uniform field $\mathbf{B}$, the

flux is just

\begin{equation*}

\pi r'^2B.

\end{equation*}

If we choose our origin on an axis of symmetry, so that we can

take $\mathbf{A}$ as circumferential and a function only of $r'$, the

circulation will be

\begin{equation*}

\oint\mathbf{A}\cdot d\mathbf{s}=2\pi r'A=\pi r'^2B.

\end{equation*}

We get, as before,

\begin{equation*}

A=\frac{Br'}{2}.

\end{equation*}

In the example we have just given, we have calculated the vector

potential from the magnetic field, which is opposite to what one

normally does. In complicated problems it is usually easier to solve

for the vector potential, and then determine the magnetic field from

it. We will now show how this can be done.

[Chapter 14. The Magnetic Field in Various Situations > 14-2 The vector potential of known currents]

14-2 The vector potential of known currents

Since $\mathbf{B}$ is determined by currents, so also is $\mathbf{A}$. We want

now to find $\mathbf{A}$ in terms of the currents. We start with our basic

equation ( 14.2 ):

\begin{equation*}

c^2\nabla \times \mathbf{B}=\frac{\mathbf{j}}{\epsilon_0},

\end{equation*}

which means, of course, that

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:12}

c^2\nabla \times (\FLPcurl{\mathbf{A})}=\frac{\mathbf{j}}{\epsilon_0}.

\end{equation}

This equation is for magnetostatics what the equation

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:13}

\nabla \cdot \nabla \phi=-\frac{\rho}{\epsilon_0}

\end{equation}

was for electrostatics.

Our equation ( 14.12 ) for the vector potential looks even

more like that for $\phi$ if we rewrite $\nabla \times (\FLPcurl{\mathbf{A})}$

using the vector identity Eq. ( 2.58 ):

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:14}

\nabla \times (\FLPcurl{\mathbf{A})}=\nabla (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A})-\nabla^2\mathbf{A}.

\end{equation}

Since we have chosen to make $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}=0$ (and now you see why),

Eq. ( 14.12 ) becomes

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:15}

\nabla^2\mathbf{A}=-\frac{\mathbf{j}}{\epsilon_0 c^2}.

\end{equation}

This vector equation means, of course, three equations:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:16}

\nabla^2A_x=-\frac{j_x}{\epsilon_0 c^2},\quad

\nabla^2A_y=-\frac{j_y}{\epsilon_0 c^2},\quad

\nabla^2A_z=-\frac{j_z}{\epsilon_0 c^2}.

\end{equation}

\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}

\nabla^2A_x&=-\frac{j_x}{\epsilon_0 c^2},\\[1ex]

\nabla^2A_y&=-\frac{j_y}{\epsilon_0 c^2},\\[1ex]

\nabla^2A_z&=-\frac{j_z}{\epsilon_0 c^2}.

\end{aligned}

\label{Eq:II:14:16}

\end{equation}

And each of these equations is mathematically identical to

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:17}

\nabla^2\phi=-\frac{\rho}{\epsilon_0}.

\end{equation}

All we have learned about solving for potentials when $\rho$ is known

can be used for solving for each component of $\mathbf{A}$ when $\mathbf{j}$ is

known!

We have seen in Chapter  4 that a general solution for the

electrostatic equation ( 14.17 ) is

\begin{equation*}

\phi(1)=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\int\frac{\rho(2)\,dV_2}{r_{12}}.

\end{equation*}

So we know immediately that a general solution for $A_x$ is

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:18}

A_x(1)=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\int\frac{j_x(2)\,dV_2}{r_{12}},

\end{equation}

and similarly for $A_y$ and $A_z$. (Figure  14-2 will

remind you of our conventions for $r_{12}$ and $dV_2$.) We can combine

the three solutions in the vector form

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:14:19}

\mathbf{A}(1)=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\int\frac{\mathbf{j}(2)\,dV_2}{r_{12}},

\end{equation}

(You can verify if you wish, by direct differentiation of components,

that this integral for $\mathbf{A}$ satisfies $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{A}=0$ so long

as $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{j}=0$, which, as we saw, must happen for steady

currents.)

Fig. 14-2. The vector potential $\mathbf{A}$ at point $1$ is given by an

integral over the current elements $\mathbf{j}\,dV$ at all points $2$.

