\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:17:16}

\text{Power from a generator}=\mathcal{E} I.

\end{equation}

When there is a current in the coil of the generator, there will also

be mechanical forces on it. In fact, we know that the torque on the

coil is proportional to its magnetic moment, to the magnetic field

strength $B$, and to the sine of the angle between. The magnetic

moment is the current in the coil times its area. Therefore the torque

