\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:17:17}

\tau=NISB\sin\theta.

\end{equation}

The rate at which mechanical work must be done to keep the coil

rotating is the angular velocity $\omega$ times the torque:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:17:18}

\frac{d W}{d t}=\omega\tau=\omega NISB\sin\theta.

\end{equation}

Comparing this equation with Eq. ( 17.14 ), we see that

the rate of mechanical work required to rotate the coil against the

magnetic forces is just equal to $\mathcal{E} I$, the rate at which

electrical energy is delivered by the emf of the generator. All of the

mechanical energy used up in the generator appears as electrical

energy in the circuit.

