We can now get the magnetic field from $\mathbf{B}=\nabla \times \mathbf{A}$.

Since $\dot{\mathbf{p}}$ is totally in the $z$-direction, $\mathbf{A}$ has only a

$z$-component; there are only two nonzero derivatives in the curl. So

$B_x=\partial A_z / \partial y$ and $B_y=-\partial A_z / \partial x$. Let’s first look at $B_x$:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:21:19}

B_x=\frac{\partial A_z}{\partial y}=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\,

\frac{\dot{p}(t-r/c)}{r}.

\end{equation}

To carry out the differentiation, we must remember that

$r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$, so

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:21:20}

B_x=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\dot{p}(t-r/c)\,\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\,

\biggl(\frac{1}{r}\biggr)+\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\frac{1}{r}\,

\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\,\dot{p}(t-r/c).

\end{equation}

\begin{align}

B_x=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\dot{p}(t-r/c)\,&\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\,

\biggl(\frac{1}{r}\biggr)\,+\notag\\[1ex]

\label{Eq:II:21:20}

\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\frac{1}{r}\,

&\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\,\dot{p}(t-r/c).

\end{align}

Remembering that $\partial r / \partial y=y/r$, the first term gives

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:21:21}

-\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\frac{y\dot{p}(t-r/c)}{r^3},

\end{equation}

which drops off as $1/r^2$ like the potential of a static dipole

(because $y/r$ is constant for a given direction).

The second term in Eq. ( 21.20 ) gives us the new

effects. Carrying out the differentiation, we get

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:21:22}

-\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0 c^2}\,\frac{y}{cr^2}\,\ddot{p}(t-r/c),

\end{equation}

where $\ddot{p}$ means, of course, the second derivative of $p$ with

respect to $t$. This term, which comes from differentiating the

numerator, is responsible for radiation. First, it describes a field

which decreases with distance only as $1/r$. Second, it depends on the

acceleration of the charge. You can begin to see how we are

going to get a result like Eq. ( 21.1´ ), which describes

the radiation of light.

