Integrating with respect to $t$ just removes one dot from each of

the $\mathbf{p}$’s, so

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:21:25}

\phi(\mathbf{r},t)=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\,

\frac{[\mathbf{p}+(r/c)\dot{\mathbf{p}}]_{t-r/c}\cdot\mathbf{r}}{r^3}.

\end{equation}

(The constant of integration would correspond to some superposed

