\mathbf{B}\cdot(\mathbf{E}\times\nabla_E)=

-\mathbf{B}\cdot(\nabla_E\times\mathbf{E}).

\end{equation*}

Now it is in a conventional order, so we can return to the usual

notation. Equation ( 27.10 ) is equivalent to

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:11}

\nabla \cdot (\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{E})=

\mathbf{E}\cdot(\nabla \times \mathbf{B})-\mathbf{B}\cdot(\nabla \times \mathbf{E}).

\end{equation}

(A quicker way would have been to use components in this special case,

but it was worth taking the time to show you the mathematical

trick. You probably won’t see it anywhere else, and it is very good

for unlocking vector

algebra

from the rules about the order of terms

with derivatives.)

We now return to our energy conservation discussion and use our new

result, Eq. ( 27.11 ), to transform the $\nabla \times \mathbf{B}$ term

of Eq. ( 27.7 ). That energy equation becomes

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:12}

\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{j}=\epsilon_0 c^2\nabla \cdot (\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{E})+\epsilon_0 c^2

\mathbf{B}\cdot(\nabla \times \mathbf{E})-\frac{\partial}{\partial t}(\frac{1}{2}\epsilon_0

\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{E}).

\end{equation}

\begin{align}

\label{Eq:II:27:12}

\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{j}=\,&\epsilon_0 c^2\nabla \cdot (\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{E})\,+\\

&\epsilon_0 c^2\mathbf{B}\cdot(\nabla \times \mathbf{E})\,-\frac{\partial}{\partial t}(\frac{1}{2}\epsilon_0

\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{E}).\notag

\end{align}

Now you see, we’re almost finished. We have one term which is a nice

derivative with respect to $t$ to use for $u$ and another that is a

beautiful divergence to represent $\mathbf{S}$. Unfortunately, there is the

center term left over, which is neither a divergence nor a derivative

with respect to $t$. So we almost made it, but not quite. After some

thought, we look back at the differential equations of

Maxwell and

discover that $\nabla \times \mathbf{E}$ is, fortunately, equal

to $-\partial \mathbf{B} / \partial t$, which means that we can turn the extra term into

something that is a pure time derivative:

\begin{equation*}

\mathbf{B}\cdot(\nabla \times \mathbf{E})=\mathbf{B}\cdot\biggl(

-\frac{\partial \mathbf{B}}{\partial t}\biggr)=-\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\biggl(

\frac{\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{B}}{2}\biggr).

\end{equation*}

Now we have exactly what we want. Our energy equation reads

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:13}

\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{j}=\nabla \cdot (\epsilon_0 c^2\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{E})-

\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\biggl(\frac{\epsilon_0 c^2}{2}\,\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{B}+

\frac{\epsilon_0}{2}\,\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{E}\biggr),

\end{equation}

\begin{align}

\label{Eq:II:27:13}

\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{j}=\;&\nabla \cdot (\epsilon_0 c^2\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{E})\,-\\[1ex]

&\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\biggl(\frac{\epsilon_0 c^2}{2}\,\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{B}+

\frac{\epsilon_0}{2}\,\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{E}\biggr),\notag

\end{align}

which is exactly like Eq. ( 27.6 ), if we make the

definitions

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:14}

u=\frac{\epsilon_0}{2}\,\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{E}+

\frac{\epsilon_0 c^2}{2}\,\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{B}

\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:15}

\mathbf{S}=\epsilon_0 c^2\mathbf{E}\times\mathbf{B}.

\end{equation}

(Reversing the cross product makes the signs come out right.)

Our program was successful. We have an expression for the energy

[Chapter 27. Field Energy and Field Momentum > 27-5 Examples of energy flow]

27-5 Examples of energy flow

Fig. 27-2. The vectors $\mathbf{E}$, $\mathbf{B}$, and $\mathbf{S}$ for a light wave.

Our formula for the energy flow vector $\mathbf{S}$ is something quite

new. We want now to see how it works in some special cases and also to

see whether it checks out with anything that we knew before. The first

example we will take is light. In a light wave we have an $\mathbf{E}$

vector and a $\mathbf{B}$ vector at right angles to each other and to the

direction of the wave propagation. (See Fig.  27-2 .) In

an electromagnetic wave, the magnitude of $\mathbf{B}$ is equal to

$1/c$ times the magnitude of $\mathbf{E}$, and since they are at right angles,

\begin{equation*}

|\mathbf{E|\times\mathbf{B}}=\frac{E^2}{c}.

\end{equation*}

Therefore, for light, the flow of energy per unit area per second is

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:16}

S=\epsilon_0 cE^2.

\end{equation}

For a light wave in which $E=E_0\cos\omega(t-x/c)$, the average rate

of energy flow per unit area, $\langle S \rangle$—which is called the

“intensity” of the light—is the mean value of the square of the

electric field times $\epsilon_0 c$:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:27:17}

\text{Intensity} = \langle S \rangle = \epsilon_0 c\langle E^2 \rangle.

\end{equation}

Believe it or not, we have already derived this result in

Section  31-5 of Vol. I, when we were studying light. We

can believe that it is right because it also checks against something

