36-2 The field $\mathbf{H}$

Next, we want to insert the current as written in

Eq. ( 36.10 ) into Maxwell’s

equations. We get

\begin{equation*}

c^2\nabla \times \mathbf{B}=\frac{\mathbf{j}}{\epsilon_0}+\frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t}=

\frac{1}{\epsilon_0}\biggl(

\mathbf{j}_{\text{cond}}+\nabla \times \mathbf{M}+\frac{\partial \mathbf{P}}{\partial t}

\biggr)+\frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t}.

\end{equation*}

\begin{align*}

c^2\nabla \times \mathbf{B}&=\frac{\mathbf{j}}{\epsilon_0}+\frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t}\\[1ex]

&=\frac{1}{\epsilon_0}\biggl(\!

\mathbf{j}_{\text{cond}}\!+\!\nabla \times \mathbf{M}\!+\!\frac{\partial \mathbf{P}}{\partial t}

\!\biggr)\!+\!\frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t}.

\end{align*}

We can move the term in $\mathbf{M}$ to the left-hand side:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:36:11}

c^2\nabla \times \biggl(\mathbf{B}-\frac{\mathbf{M}{\epsilon_0 c^2}\biggr)}=\,

\frac{\mathbf{j}_{\text{cond}}}{\epsilon_0}+\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\biggl(

\mathbf{E}+\frac{\mathbf{P}}{\epsilon_0}\biggr).

\end{equation}

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:36:11}

c^2\nabla \times \biggl(\!\mathbf{B}\!-\!\frac{\mathbf{M}{\epsilon_0 c^2}\!\biggr)}\!=

\frac{\mathbf{j}_{\text{cond}}}{\epsilon_0}\!+\!\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\biggl(\!

\mathbf{E}\!+\!\frac{\mathbf{P}}{\epsilon_0}\!\biggr).

\end{equation}

As we remarked in Chapter  32 , many people like to

write $(\mathbf{E}+\mathbf{P}/\epsilon_0)$ as a new vector field $\mathbf{D}/\epsilon_0$. Similarly,

it is often convenient to write $(\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{M}/\epsilon_0 c^2)$ as a single

vector field. We choose to define a new vector field $\mathbf{H}$ by

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:36:12}

\mathbf{H}=\mathbf{B}-\frac{\mathbf{M}}{\epsilon_0 c^2}.

\end{equation}

Then Eq. ( 36.11 ) becomes

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:36:13}

\epsilon_0 c^2\nabla \times \mathbf{H}=\mathbf{j}_{\text{cond}}+\frac{\partial \mathbf{D}}{\partial t}.

\end{equation}

It looks simple, but all the complexity is just hidden in the letters

$\mathbf{D}$ and $\mathbf{H}$.

Now we have to give you a warning. Most people who use the mks units

have chosen to use a different definition of $\mathbf{H}$. Calling

their field $\mathbf{H}'$ (of course, they still call it $\mathbf{H}$

without the prime), it is defined by

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:36:14}

\mathbf{H}'=\epsilon_0 c^2\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{M}.

\end{equation}

(Also, they usually write $\epsilon_0 c^2$ as a new number $1/\mu_0$; then

they have one more constant to keep track of!) With this definition,

Eq. ( 36.13 ) looks even simpler:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:36:15}

\nabla \times \mathbf{H}'=\mathbf{j}_{\text{cond}}+\frac{\partial \mathbf{D}}{\partial t}.

\end{equation}

But the difficulties with this definition of $\mathbf{H}'$ are, first, that

it doesn’t agree with the definition of people who don’t use the mks

units, and second, that it makes $\mathbf{H}'$ and $\mathbf{B}$ have different

units. We think it is more convenient for $\mathbf{H}$ to have the same units

as $\mathbf{B}$—rather than the units of $\mathbf{M}$, as $\mathbf{H}'$ does. But if

you are going to be an engineer and work on the design of transformers,

magnets, and such, you will have to watch out. You will find many books

