39-3 The motions in an elastic body

Fig. 39-5. A small volume element $V$ bounded by the surface $A$.

We have pointed out that for an elastic body in equilibrium the

internal stresses adjust themselves to make the energy a minimum. Now

we take a look at what happens when the internal forces are not

in equilibrium. Let’s say we have a small piece of the material inside

some surface $A$. See Fig.  39-5 . If the piece is in equilibrium, the

total force $\mathbf{F}$ acting on it must be zero. We can think of this

force as being made up of two parts. There could be one part due to

“external” forces like gravity, which act from a distance on the

matter in the piece to produce a force per unit

volume $\mathbf{f}_{\text{ext}}$. The total external force $\mathbf{F}_{\text{ext}}$ is

the integral of $\mathbf{f}_{\text{ext}}$ over the volume of the piece:

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:23}

\mathbf{F}_{\text{ext}}=\int\mathbf{f}_{\text{ext}}\,dV.

\end{equation}

In equilibrium, this force would be balanced by the total

force $\mathbf{F}_{\text{int}}$ from the neighboring material which acts across

the surface $A$. When the piece is not in equilibrium—if it

is moving—the sum of the internal and external forces is equal to

the mass times the acceleration. We would have

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:24}

\mathbf{F}_{\text{ext}}+\mathbf{F}_{\text{int}}=

\int\rho\ddot{\mathbf{r}}\,dV,

\end{equation}

where $\rho$ is the density of the material, and $\ddot{\mathbf{r}}$ is its acceleration. We can now combine Eqs. ( 39.23 ) and ( 39.24 ), writing

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:25}

\mathbf{F}_{\text{int}}=\int_v(-\mathbf{f}_{\text{ext}}+\rho\ddot{\mathbf{r}})\,dV.

\end{equation}

We will simplify our writing by defining

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:26}

\mathbf{f}=-\mathbf{f}_{\text{ext}}+\rho\ddot{\mathbf{r}}.

\end{equation}

Then Eq. ( 39.25 ) is written

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:27}

\mathbf{F}_{\text{int}}=\int_v\mathbf{f}\,dV.

\end{equation}

What we have called $\mathbf{F}_{\text{int}}$ is related to the stresses in

the material. The stress tensor $S_{ij}$ was defined

(Chapter  31 ) so that the $x$-component of the force $dF$

across a surface element $da$, whose unit normal is $\mathbf{n}$, is given by

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:28}

dF_x=(S_{xx}n_x+S_{xy}n_y+S_{xz}n_z)\,da.

\end{equation}

The $x$-component of $\mathbf{F}_{\text{int}}$ on our little piece is then

the integral of $dF_x$ over the surface. Substituting this into the

$x$-component of Eq. ( 39.27 ), we get

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:29}

\int_A(S_{xx}n_x+S_{xy}n_y+S_{xz}n_z)\,da=\int_vf_x\,dV.

\end{equation}

