$\nabla \times \FLPcurl{\mathbf{u}}$, which is also a vector. But remember that

$\nabla \times \FLPcurl{\mathbf{u}}$ is the same thing

as $\nabla (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})-\nabla^2\mathbf{u}$, so it is a linear

combination of the two terms we have. Adding it would add nothing

new. We have proved once more that isotropic material has only two

elastic constants.

For the equation of motion of the material, we can

set ( 39.32 ) equal to $\rho\,\partial^2\mathbf{u}/\partial

t^2$—neglecting for now any body forces like gravity—and get

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:33}

\rho\,\frac{\partial^2\mathbf{u}}{\partial t^2}=

(\lambda+\mu)\,\nabla (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})+\mu\,\nabla^2\mathbf{u}.

\end{equation}

It looks something like the wave equation we had in electromagnetism,

except that there is an additional complicating term. For materials

whose elastic properties are everywhere the same we can see what the

general solutions look like in the following way. You will remember

that any vector field can be written as the sum of two vectors: one

whose divergence is zero, and the other whose curl is zero. In other

words, we can put

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:34}

\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2,

\end{equation}

where

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:35}

\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_1=0,\quad

\nabla \times \mathbf{u}_2=\mathbf{0}.

\end{equation}

Substituting $\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2$ for $\mathbf{u}$ in ( 39.33 ), we

get

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:36}

\rho\,\partial^2/\partial t^2[\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2]=

(\lambda+\mu)\,\nabla (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_2)+

\mu\,\nabla^2(\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2).

\end{equation}

\begin{align}

\rho\,\partial^2/&\partial t^2[\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2]=\notag\\[1ex]

\label{Eq:II:39:36}

&(\lambda+\mu)\,\nabla (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_2)+

\mu\,\nabla^2(\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2).

\end{align}

We can eliminate $\mathbf{u}_1$ by taking the divergence of this equation,

\begin{equation*}

\rho\,\partial^2/\partial t^2(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_2)=

(\lambda+\mu)\,\nabla^2(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_2)+

\mu\,\nabla \cdot \nabla^2(\mathbf{u}_2).

\end{equation*}

\begin{align*}

\rho\,\partial^2/&\partial t^2(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_2)=\notag\\[1ex]

&(\lambda+\mu)\,\nabla^2(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_2)+

\mu\,\nabla \cdot \nabla^2(\mathbf{u}_2).

\end{align*}

Since the operators ($\nabla^2$) and ($\FLPdiv{}$) can be

interchanged, we can factor out the divergence to get

\begin{equation}

\label{Eq:II:39:37}

\nabla \cdot \{\rho\,\partial^2\mathbf{u}_2/\partial t^2-

(\lambda+2\mu)\,\nabla^2\mathbf{u}_2\}=0.

\end{equation}

